# Enforce the `engines` range in package.json instead of warning about it.
#
# This is here for one specific failure. npm 10 and 11 disagree about which
# entries a lockfile must contain: adding a dependency under npm 11 left
# `nitro`'s `unstorage` requirement for `lru-cache@^11` resolved only by a
# hoisted 5.1.1 entry, which npm 10 then rejected with "Missing:
# lru-cache@11.5.2 from lock file". Every CI job died at its first step, and
# `npm ci` passed on the machine that wrote the lock.
#
# It has to be the engine check rather than a `preinstall` script: npm writes
# the lockfile before it runs lifecycle scripts, so a script can only report a
# mismatch that is already on disk. Verified by restoring the lockfile,
# running the install a script would refuse, and finding it stripped again.
#
# Deployment installs opt out — see `installCommand` in vercel.json. Nothing
# that only runs `npm ci` can corrupt a lockfile, so the guard is pure cost
# there, and it failed the Vercel build when it applied.
engine-strict=true
